{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject when it is installed before the code that could pass it is written, and the installation ritual forces failures to be visible as failures rather than silently absorbed by the test's own guard logic; the 3-of-26 result is the residue of tests whose guards checked declared data rather than added behavior, so they described what the author already believed instead of constraining what the code could become.","counterconditions":["If the mutation is syntactic rather than behavioral, declared-data guards may constrain adequately.","If the registry is rebuilt after each mutation instead of before, installation-first ordering is irrelevant.","If the author writes the test and the mutation in the same session, the test is more likely to describe the author's current belief than to constrain the code.","If the test suite is small enough that a human reviews every passing case, the guard ontology matters less."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01cc9b7f77380bf9","quote":"\"| GAP REG-1 | 0/6 |\", \"| 6/6 | `src/poietics/pff/registry.py` |\"; zoo/report/10_MUTATION_TESTING.md"},{"block":"057f5d8af80d2d12","quote":"\"THE THREE SURVIVORS ARE ONE BUG IN TWO COSTUMES: a guard that checks DECLARED DATA does not see ADDED BEHAVIOUR.\""},{"block":"03be16ddea3ab3e2","quote":"a process guard that never fires on its author is probably not a guard"}],"mechanism":"Installation-first ordering creates a registry of expected failures. When a mutation lands, the registry forces a decision: either the mutation is declared as the new passing state or the test must fire. Tests that only inspect declared structure (names, signatures, literal fields) cannot see behavioral mutations, so they report 'caught' without constraining anything. The 3-of-26 survivors are exactly the cases where the guard's ontology (declared data) is disjoint from the mutation's ontology (added behavior).","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.42},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject only when the test's failure path is itself subject to mutation testing; otherwise the test can be a description wearing a guard's uniform, and the asymmetry (1/9 vs 4/4 to 6/7) arises because the failing-mutation ritual for compile.py was applied to the compiler's own guard logic while the other modules' guards were never shown a fail-first installation.","counterconditions":["If the mutation testing framework excludes the test's own guard logic from mutation, this asymmetry disappears.","If compile.py's lost mutation was in a different file from the other 9, the result is confounded.","If the 4/4 to 6/7 modules had fewer mutation opportunities per guard, the ratio is an artifact of registry size.","If the author ran the mutation suite in a different order for different modules, installation-first ordering is not the cause."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"002779d3ed9dd0a6","quote":"The content-edit hole was in the code but not plainly in the record"},{"block":"01ccc04a460b08fc","quote":"It is one repository, one author, one week, and the mutations were proposed by models that had read the acceptance record."}],"mechanism":"Shown-to-fail-first installation forces the test's own failure path to be exercised. In compile.py, the one mutation that was lost was lost because the guard that should have caught it was itself the mutated subject. In the other modules, 4/4 to 6/7 guards fired because they were installed after the code they guarded, so the mutations were caught by guards that had never been shown to fail. The 3-of-26 distribution is a property of which guard was installed first, not of which code was mutated.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.35},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The 3-of-26 result is not evidence about tests at all but about the author's attention budget: a test constrains its subject only when the author is willing to treat its failure as a defect in the test rather than a defect in the code, and the three survivors mark the points where the author's care ran out.","counterconditions":["If the mutation suite was run automatically without author intervention, attention budget cannot explain the distribution.","If the three survivors are in the same file as caught mutations, attention budget is less plausible.","If the author wrote all tests before writing any code, there is no 'done' module to rationalize.","If the registry records timestamps of test edits, we can check whether the survivors were edited less recently."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01ccc04a460b08fc","quote":"It is one repository, one author, one week, and the mutations were proposed by models that had read the acceptance record."},{"block":"09208b033e714960","quote":"Eighteen field reports document its failure modes, and every remedy is a mechanism that fails loudly - not one is an intention to"}],"mechanism":"The same author, same week, same care produced a distribution (1/9, 4/4, 6/7) that correlates with file size and proximity to the author's current focus, not with any property of the tests. When a mutation lands in a module the author is actively thinking about, the author notices the failure and fixes the test. When it lands in a module the author considers done, the author rationalizes the failure as 'the test still describes the right thing'. The 3-of-26 survivors are the modules the author had stopped constraining.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.28},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject when the test's assertion is a necessary condition for the subject's accepted behavior, not a restatement of the subject's implementation; the 3-of-26 result is the proportion of tests whose assertions were restatements of the code they were supposed to constrain, and the 1/9 vs 4/4 to 6/7 spread is the ratio of restatements to necessary conditions in each module.","counterconditions":["If the mutation changes the property that the test asserts, even a restatement will fail.","If the test asserts a necessary condition but the mutation is in a different branch, the test may still pass.","If the author intended the test as a restatement (e.g., a snapshot test), this is not a failure of the test.","If the 3-of-26 survivors are all in the same category of assertion, the ratio is an artifact of test style, not test quality."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"057f5d8af80d2d12","quote":"\"THE THREE SURVIVORS ARE ONE BUG IN TWO COSTUMES: a guard that checks DECLARED DATA does not see ADDED BEHAVIOUR.\""}],"mechanism":"When a test asserts something the code already does, the test describes the code. When a test asserts something the code must do but might not, the test constrains. A mutation that changes behavior without changing the restated property is invisible to a describing test. The 3-of-26 survivors are the tests where the assertion was 'the code does X' rather than 'the code must do X, and X is the only acceptable behavior'. The 1/9 lost mutation in compile.py is the one case where the assertion was a necessary condition but the mutation changed the necessity itself.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.22},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A test constrains its subject only under an installation ritual that requires the test to fail before the subject exists, and the 3-of-26 result is the number of tests that were never shown to fail; compile.py's 1/9 is the only module where the shown-to-fail-first installation was actually performed, while the 4/4 to 6/7 modules were tested after the fact, so their guards described the code rather than constraining it.","counterconditions":["If the test is written before the code but the code is written to make the test pass, the test still constrains.","If the test is written after the code but the author genuinely does not know what the code does, the test can still constrain.","If the 4/4 to 6/7 modules had their tests written first, the installation-order explanation fails.","If the registry does not record test creation time, this claim is untestable from the available evidence."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"0a5a41977d58bdb4","quote":"Install first authority, audit it, and revise the pin to v0.2"},{"block":"03be16ddea3ab3e2","quote":"a process guard that never fires on its author is probably not a guard"}],"mechanism":"The installation ritual is the key variable, not the test's content. When a test is written before the code, its failure is the specification. When it is written after, its failure is a bug report. The 3-of-26 survivors are the tests written after the code they guard, so they were never shown to fail and therefore never constrained anything. The 1/9 lost mutation in compile.py is the one case where the shown-to-fail-first installation was performed but the mutation was in the installation ritual itself, not in the subject.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.18},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The 3-of-26 result is a confound of registry size: a test constrains its subject only when the registry of expected failures is small enough that each mutation is a decision, and the 1/9 vs 4/4 to 6/7 spread is the ratio of mutations to registry entries in each module, not a property of the tests themselves.","counterconditions":["If the registry is rebuilt from scratch for each mutation, registry size is irrelevant.","If the 3-of-26 survivors are in modules with small registries, this explanation fails.","If the mutation testing framework uses a fixed threshold for 'caught' rather than a registry, this mechanism does not apply.","If the author manually reviewed every mutation result, registry size cannot explain the distribution."],"evidence_refs":[{"block":"01ccc04a460b08fc","quote":"It is one repository, one author, one week, and the mutations were proposed by models that had read the acceptance record."},{"block":"01cc9b7f77380bf9","quote":"\"| GAP REG-1 | 0/6 |\", \"| 6/6 | `src/poietics/pff/registry.py` |\"; zoo/report/10_MUTATION_TESTING.md"}],"mechanism":"The mutation testing framework works by maintaining a registry of expected failures. When the registry is small, each mutation is a decision: is this the new passing state or is the test wrong? When the registry is large, mutations are absorbed into the noise. The 3-of-26 survivors are the mutations that landed in modules where the registry was large enough to absorb them. The 1/9 lost mutation in compile.py is the one case where the registry was small enough that the mutation was a decision, but the decision was made in the wrong direction.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.12}],"simulation_proposals":[]}